# 14. ANIMATION SPECIFICATIONS

Animations are important, but restraint is essential.

Every animation should serve a purpose.

## A. Page transitions

When switching between major sections:
- Fade out the current content.
- Move the new content upward slightly.
- Fade the new content in.
- Duration: approximately 250–400ms.
- Use smooth easing.

Avoid dramatic sliding transitions.

## B. Navigation hover

When hovering over navigation:
- Underline appears or expands.
- Slight text colour change.
- Duration: approximately 150–200ms.

On mobile, use clear tap feedback instead.

## C. Rotating announcements

- Fade the old announcement out.
- Replace the text.
- Fade the new announcement in.
- Duration: approximately 300–500ms.

## D. Countdown

- Update every second.
- Use subtle number transitions.
- Avoid aggressive bouncing or flashing.
- Keep digits aligned to prevent layout shifts.

## E. Coffee machine

Create a brewing sequence:
1. Button press feedback.
2. Machine status changes.
3. Brewing indicator activates.
4. Subtle steam or progress animation.
5. Result appears.

Total duration: approximately 1–2 seconds.

## F. Activity results

When generating a result:
- Briefly fade out the previous result.
- Reveal the new result.
- Optionally use a small upward movement.
- Avoid excessive loading animations.

## G. Archive documents

When opening a document:
- Fade in the document.
- Slight upward movement.
- Optional subtle stamp appearance.
- No unnecessary dramatic effects.

## H. Rare chaos events

Occasional effects may include:
- Brief text distortion.
- A subtle horizontal displacement.
- A temporary status interruption.
- A short-lived notification.

Do not use continuous glitch animations.

## I. Birthday mode

Use subtle celebratory particles or minimal visual changes.

The website must still look like the Bureau.

## J. Reduced motion

Respect `prefers-reduced-motion`.

When enabled:
- Disable nonessential movement.
- Reduce transitions.
- Remove unnecessary particles.
- Keep all functionality intact.

